ts justify moving toward the polling rather than the ratings consensus. Markets still price Graham around an 85% favorite, suggesting the tight polls may overstate the shift.", "watch": "Whether post-Labor-day polling from higher-quality, less partisan pollsters confirms the single-digit race or reverts toward the state's Republican lean.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Darline Graham (R) is running."]}, "overview": "South Carolina's Senate race has emerged as an unexpected contest. Incumbent Darline Graham, an interim appointee, faces Democrat Annie Andrews, a well-funded challenger who has raised over $10 million to Graham's roughly $350,000. Recent polls from both parties' sponsors show the race within a few points, a notable shift in a state that leans Republican by eight points.\n\nMost expert ratings still place the race at Likely R or Lean R, and prediction markets give Graham roughly an 85% chance of holding the seat. The combination of strong challenger fundraising, competitive polling, and a favorable national environment keeps this closer than typical South Carolina races, but the incumbent's structural advantages and the state's partisan lean still favor her heading into the final weeks.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1565, "margin": -5.15}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2122, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2115, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2143, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1474, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2135, "margin": -4.0}]}